  "answer": "**Where the event is:** the `tracing::trace!` at `tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:561-565`, inside `Waiter::assign_permits` (fn at line 551). It sits in `self.ctx.async_op_span.in_scope(...)` and is compiled only under `cfg(all(tokio_unstable, feature = \"tracing\"))`. Its target is `runtime::resource::async_op::state_update`. It fires with `permits_obtained = assign, permits.op = \"add\"`. The subscriber's callback runs inside this event.\n\n**Why a panic there leaves a completed waiter in the queue:**\n\n- `assign_permits` first does `state.compare_exchange(curr, next)` (line 556). On success the waiter's permit count is already decremented, and `*n -= assign` has run (line 558). Only after that does the trace event fire (line 561), and only then does `return next == 0` (line 567).\n- `add_permits_locked` (lines 314-327) calls `waiters.queue.last()` and then `waiter.assign_permits(&mut rem)`. It only calls `queue.pop_back()` (line 327) after `assign_permits` returns `true`.\n- If the subscriber panics inside the trace event, `assign_permits` unwinds before it returns. The waiter's state is already 0, so it counts as complete. It is still linked in `waiters.queue`, because the `pop_back` at line 327 never ran, and its waker was not taken.\n- The panic unwinds out of `add_permits_locked` and releases the mutex guard. The next operation on the queue, such as `Acquire::drop` or another `add_permits`, then sees a completed waiter. That waiter is a pointer into the `Acquire` future's pinned memory, which the owner may free once it observes the permits. The queue then holds a dangling pointer. This is the use-after-free.\n\n**Uncertainty:** I read only the code at the pinned commit. I did not read issue #8541, so the last step (who frees the node and who dereferences it next) is my inference from the code, not something the issue confirmed. I did not trace `Acquire::drop` to confirm the exact dereferencing path.",
